 "last_activity": "2012-04-18T17:52:34Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "bov6qaTEIScJ",
   "from": "Matthew Krachey",
   "author": "057bfe4d4b636b43",
   "date": "2012-04-18T14:42:08Z",
   "subject": "fitList functionality",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi,<p>I&#39;m working on some raster occupancy maps based on model averaged predictions of some excessive model mining. In general, the fitList functionality seems great, however, I would like to be able to truncate the fitList to the models up to 95% of AIC weight, and from here, generate the model-averaged coefficients so I can simply apply the coefficient values to a rasterBrick to generate predicted values. <p>So my questions are 1) is there a way to subset the fitList to either an AIC cut-off or to fitList sorted models 1-X? Second, coef(fittedList) generates the coefficient values for each model. Is there a method to generate the model-averaged coefficients instead of model-averaged predictions?<p>Matthew</p></p></p>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "JeaOID7dMXsJ",
   "from": "Dan Linden",
   "author": "527d9b3ec7deff2d",
   "date": "2012-04-18T16:19:55Z",
   "subject": "Re: fitList functionality",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "The following code would identify the poor models (those beyond 95% of the cumulative AIC weight) and set them to NULL in your model list:<br><br><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Arial;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\"><span style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:16px;line-height:19px;text-align:-webkit-left;white-space:pre-wrap\"><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:12pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-top-style:none;border-right-style:none;border-bottom-style:none;border-left-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Arial;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\"><span style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:16px;line-height:19px;text-align:-webkit-left;white-space:pre-wrap\"><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:12pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-top-style:none;border-right-style:none;border-bottom-style:none;border-left-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"color:blue\">poor.models &lt;- </span><span style=\"color:blue\">modSel.object@Full$model[</span><span style=\"color:blue\">which(modSel.object@Full$cumltvWt&gt;0.95),]</span><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Arial;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\"><span style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:16px;line-height:19px;text-align:-webkit-left;white-space:pre-wrap\"></span></span><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Arial;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\"><span style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:16px;line-height:19px;text-align:-webkit-left;white-space:pre-wrap\"></span></span><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Arial;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\"><span style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:16px;line-height:19px;text-align:-webkit-left;white-space:pre-wrap\"></span></span></pre></span></span></pre></span></span><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Arial;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\"><span style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:16px;line-height:19px;text-align:-webkit-left;white-space:pre-wrap\"><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:12pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-top-style:none;border-right-style:none;border-bottom-style:none;border-left-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"color:blue\">modelList.object@fits[poor.models</span><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Arial;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\"><span style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:16px;line-height:19px;text-align:-webkit-left;white-space:pre-wrap\"></span></span><span style=\"color:blue\">]&lt;-NULL<br></span><br><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\">I&#39;m not entirely sure why you would do this, but this modified list could be used to generate new model-averaged predictions for just the subset of models.  I tried doing this to a small set of models and found that the predictions from a modelList where all but 1 model was removed were the same as when that single model itself was specified, yet the confidence intervals were different:<br><br></span></font><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Arial;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:auto;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\"><span style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:16px;line-height:19px;text-align:-webkit-left;white-space:pre-wrap\"><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:12pt!important;outline-style:none;outline-width:initial;outline-color:initial;border-top-style:none;border-right-style:none;border-bottom-style:none;border-left-style:none;border-width:initial;border-color:initial;white-space:pre-wrap!important;margin-top:0px;margin-right:0px;margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:0px;line-height:1.2\"><span style=\"white-space:pre;color:blue\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:blue\">predict(fm3, type=&quot;state&quot;, newdata=newDat)\r\n</span>  Predicted         SE     lower    upper\r\n1 0.8734059 0.08488826 0.6050754 0.968816\r\n<span style=\"white-space:pre;color:blue\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:blue\">predict(modelList, type=&quot;state&quot;, newdata=newDat)\r\n</span>  Predicted         SE     lower    upper\r\n1 0.8734059 0.08488826 0.7070249 1.039787</pre></span></span><br><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\">Not sure what is going on there.  As for model-averaged coefficients, I recall reading that there are problems with trying to generate/interpret them.  I could be wrong.<br><br>-Dan</span></font> <br></pre></span></span>"
    ]
   ]
